      "writtenOn": "2026-10-29T22:52:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "128 counts hash values, not bytes. The buffers hold uint32_t, so 128 values are 512 bytes, and the smallest step of the walk (2 rows) is 1 KB.\n\nA width that\u0027s an odd multiple of 64 gives a 512 byte step, so a column uses 1/8 of the sets. Whether that hurts depends on the column length and the L2. On Zen 3 (my cpu) it is 512 KB, 8 way, 1024 sets, 1/8 of the sets hold 1024 lines. A 1440 row frame (720 reads per column) still fits, but a 2160 row frame (1080 reads) doesn\u0027t. So 128 isn\u0027t a hard limit. It\u0027s just where common sizes start to hurt. The padding fixes every width.",
      "parentUuid": "69bdd5d0_be603714",
      "revId": "978dc959d997f6886d22da561fdfe086e43558dc",
      "serverId": "e5514cf8-2d6e-3e29-adb4-24cd6dde4bf0"
    },
