      "writtenOn": "2026-10-29T22:52:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "2 rows of 8 values are exactly one 64 byte line. With an odd multiple of 8, a 2 row step is an odd number of whole lines, so a column goes through every set before it reuses one. An odd multiple of 4 also spreads the column. Just making the width odd isn\u0027t always enough, though. At 2559 wide, a 2 row step is 8 bytes short of 5 x 4 KB, so the column only moves to a new set every 8 reads.\n\nI timed the fill alone at 2560x1440 with different pads and 8 was the fastest on both machines, so I picked it. I don\u0027t have counter data that explains why 4 is a bit slower.\n\nAnother option is to switch the walk to row-major order, so we would access memory sequentially and wouldn\u0027t need any padding. But, this would slightly change the output.",
      "parentUuid": "60024914_8d07983e",
      "revId": "978dc959d997f6886d22da561fdfe086e43558dc",
      "serverId": "e5514cf8-2d6e-3e29-adb4-24cd6dde4bf0"
    },
